<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rohtak</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rohtak is a city in the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. Excavations at the nearby mound of Khokrakot have produced coin moulds of the Yaudheya republic, showing that the area was an important Yaudheya centre in the early centuries CE. It is today a district headquarters and an educational centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rohtak.jpg" alt="Rohtak" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mananjainhsr · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Institute_of_Management_Rohtak,_Academic_Block.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rohtak is the administrative headquarters of Rohtak district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>. It lies 70 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> and 250 kilometres south of the state capital Chandigarh, and forms part of the National Capital Region. The climate is hot and semi-arid, with an average annual rainfall of about 613 millimetres, 80 percent of it falling between July and September, so that farming in the district depends largely on canal irrigation and tubewells. The name is derived from an earlier form, Rohtasgarh, the fort of Rohtas, after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> chieftain associated with the early settlement. The older form Rauhītaka occurs in the 12th-century Rājataraṅgiṇī.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations at Khokhrakot have uncovered clay moulds for casting coins of the Yaudheyas, dated to the 3rd or 4th century CE, with clay seals of that and later periods and Gupta terracotta from the 4th to 6th centuries. The astronomical text Surya Siddhanta, of the 4th century CE, placed a prime meridian between Avanti (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>) and Rohitaka, taken to be Rohtak. A 7th-century statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parshvanatha">Parshvanatha</a>, the 23rd Jain tirthankara, was found at the village of Asthal Bohar, and Jain sources of the 9th century show the prominence of Parshvanatha in the area.</p><p>The town flourished until the 10th century, as coins of Samanta Deva of the Hindu Shahi found there show, and the Ghaznavids under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> conquered it between 1020 and 1030. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chahamanas</a> ruled the region in the 12th century.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ain-i-akbari">Ain-i-Akbari</a> lists Rohtak as a Mughal pargana under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> sarkar, dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a>, with a brick fort, a revenue of 8,599,270 dams and a force of 2,000 infantry and 100 cavalry. A fort in what is now Qilla Mohallah was besieged by Syed Khizer Khan in 1410 and fell only after six months. In 1762 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suraj-mal">Suraj Mal</a>, the Jat ruler of Bharatpur, sent his sons Jawahar Singh and Nahar Singh to capture what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>, and they took the Rohtak pargana. British officers lived there from 1810, and All Saints Church was completed in 1867.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rohtak had 373,133 inhabitants at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is home to Maharshi Dayanand University, founded in 1976, and to the Pandit Bhagwat Dayal Sharma Post Graduate Institute of Medical Sciences, which began as a medical college in 1960. The Baba Mast Nath monastery was founded in 1731, and the town has havelis, mosques and temples in traditional Indian and Mughal styles. It lies on national highways and has a railway junction with connections to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rohtak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Coin moulds found at <b>Rohtak</b> suggest it was an important centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/yaudheya-republic">Yaudheya republic</a></li></ul></section>
